### Inventria API
#
# Every request below is one this API actually serves. Run them top to bottom the
# first time: nothing can be received until an item and a bin exist, and nothing
# at all works until you have signed in.
#
# AUTHENTICATION
# A successful login returns the JWT as an HttpOnly cookie rather than in the
# body, so there is no token here to copy into a variable. Both the VS Code REST
# Client and Visual Studio's .http editor keep cookies for the host and attach
# them to later requests, so signing in once is enough. If your client does not,
# send the token yourself with an `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header - the
# API prefers that header when it is present, which is what keeps curl working.
#
# The `https` launch profile also listens on https://localhost:7149.

@inventria_HostAddress = http://localhost:5240

### Sign in. Seeded admin username is `admin` unless Seed:AdminUsername says
### otherwise; the password is printed once in the startup log when generated.
### Ten wrong passwords in 15 minutes locks the account, and 20 attempts in 5
### minutes from one address is answered with 429 whatever they are.
POST {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "admin",
  "password": "change-me"
}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------- MASTER ITEMS

### Every item, each with the units on hand summed across the bins holding it.
GET {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/inventory
Accept: application/json

### Create an item. SKUs are unique and compared after trimming.
POST {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/inventory/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "sku": "SKU-100",
  "name": "Steel Wrench",
  "category": "Tools"
}

### Update an item. Replace 1 with the id the create returned.
PUT {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/inventory/items/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "sku": "SKU-100",
  "name": "Steel Wrench (15mm)",
  "category": "Tools"
}

### Delete an item. Refused with 409 while stock or movement history references
### it - the foreign keys enforce that, the message explains which one is in the way.
DELETE {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/inventory/items/1

### -------------------------------------------------------------- WAREHOUSE BINS

### Every bin, ordered as a walk through the warehouse.
GET {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/warehousebins
Accept: application/json

### Create a bin. Zone/Aisle/Shelf together must be unique: two rows with the
### same address are two ids for one physical shelf.
POST {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/warehousebins
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "zone": "Electronics",
  "aisle": "A1",
  "shelf": "S3"
}

### Update a bin.
PUT {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/warehousebins/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "zone": "Electronics",
  "aisle": "A1",
  "shelf": "S4"
}

### Delete a bin. Refused while it holds stock or has movements against it.
DELETE {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/warehousebins/1

### ------------------------------------------------------------- STOCK MOVEMENTS
#
# None of these carry a `performedBy`: the API stamps movements with the username
# from the session token, so there is deliberately no field to set it with.
# Quantities must be whole numbers greater than zero.

### Receive stock into a bin.
POST {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/inventory/receive
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "itemId": 1,
  "warehouseBinId": 1,
  "quantity": 25
}

### Pick stock out of a bin. 400 when the bin does not hold that many.
POST {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/inventory/pick
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "itemId": 1,
  "warehouseBinId": 1,
  "quantity": 5
}

### Move stock between bins. Writes both legs of the move to the ledger, so a
### bin's movements still add up to its balance.
POST {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/inventory/relocate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "itemId": 1,
  "sourceBinId": 1,
  "destinationBinId": 2,
  "quantity": 10
}

### ------------------------------------------------------------------ DASHBOARDS

### Admin only: totals, category split and the last five movements.
GET {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/dashboard/admin
Accept: application/json

### Any signed-in user: units on hand, SKUs tracked, and today's receipts and picks.
GET {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/dashboard/employee
Accept: application/json

### ----------------------------------------------------------------------- USERS
#
# Admin only, all four. There is no public registration endpoint: one would let
# any caller pick their own role. The first Admin is seeded at startup.

### List accounts. Password hashes are never returned.
GET {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/users
Accept: application/json

### Create an account. Role must be exactly "Admin" or "Employee".
POST {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "emp_105",
  "password": "correct horse battery staple",
  "role": "Employee"
}

### Update an account. An empty password means "leave the current one alone".
PUT {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/users/2
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "emp_105",
  "password": "",
  "role": "Admin"
}

### Delete an account.
DELETE {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/users/2

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------- LOGOUT

### Clears the session cookie. Anonymous, so an expired session can still tidy up.
POST {{inventria_HostAddress}}/api/auth/logout

###
